  "answer": "The KX-100 cannot do this measurement, for two independent reasons. First, its frequency range is 10 MHz to 18 GHz; a 40 GHz signal is outside the band, SENS:FREQ refuses the value (error -222) and no calibrated reading is possible. Second, its resolution is fixed at 0.01 dB (FETC? returns two decimals), so 0.001 dB resolution is not available, and no setting or averaging count changes that. You will need a sensor/meter rated to 40 GHz with 0.001 dB display resolution; if you only need to see relative drift, note that even then the KX-100's noise and accuracy figures (several tenths of a percent) are far larger than 0.001 dB.",
  "quote": "A reading finer than the resolution is not available from the instrument, and a signal outside the frequency range cannot be measured with a calibrated result."
}

